{
  "id": "py-11-match",
  "track": "python",
  "unit": "py-u2",
  "order": 11,
  "title": "match and case",
  "summary": "Compare one value against several fixed values, and run the case that matches.",
  "language": "python",
  "minutes": 20,
  "visual": "memory",
  "visuals": [
    "branch",
    "memory"
  ],
  "intro": {
    "text": "When you compare **one value** against several fixed values, `match` reads more clearly than a long elif chain:\n\n```\nmatch command:\n    case \"start\":\n        print(\"Starting\")\n    case \"stop\":\n        print(\"Stopping\")\n    case _:\n        print(\"Unknown command\")\n```\n\nPython tries the cases from the top and runs the **first one that matches**. Only one case runs.\n\n- `case \"a\" | \"b\":` matches either value\n- `case _:` matches anything, so it goes last, like else\n\nmatch is good for fixed values like words and numbers. For ranges (\"more than 10\") keep using if and elif. `match` was added in Python 3.10, after the book was written.",
    "analogy": "A vending machine with labelled buttons. You press one button (the value), and the machine looks along its list of labels until one is the same, then drops that snack. The last slot, labelled \"anything else\", gives your coin back."
  },
